# What `tokei` counts here: the code that ships lark's behaviour.
#
# The definition, so a future line can be judged against it — TypeScript, TSX
# and Kotlin that ends up in a dmg or an apk, minus its tests. Everything a
# line below removes is real, committed and still not the product.
#
# tokei already honours .gitignore, so build output and node_modules are gone
# before this file is read.
#
# To see the excluded half, point tokei at it directly: `tokei scripts`,
# `tokei spikes`, or `tokei -e '!*.test.ts'` for the tests alone.

# ── tests ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bigger than the code they cover, on purpose.
*.test.ts
*.test.tsx
*.e2e.ts
**/testing/
**/fixtures/
**/__fixtures__/

# ── harnesses and build tooling ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The acceptance suites, the dependency guards, the device drivers, the
# packaging scripts. They verify and produce the product; none of them is in
# it. `**/scripts/` and not `/scripts/`: packages/*/scripts/ is the same thing.
**/scripts/
justfile
*.config.js
*.config.mjs
*.config.ts

# ── spikes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kept deliberately (media-protocol is M4's reference, mobile-foundation still
# hosts the device-driving tools), but a spike is an answered question.
/spikes/

# ── generated native project ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CNG writes these from app.json and the config plugins; `expo prebuild` can
# recreate every line, so counting them counts the generator twice. The
# HAND-WRITTEN native code is apps/mobile/modules/ and apps/mobile/plugins/,
# and both stay — they decide what the apk contains.
/apps/mobile/android/
/apps/mobile/ios/

# ── manifests, config and prose ──────────────────────────────────────────────
*.json
*.yml
*.yaml
*.xml
*.md
/docs/
/vendor/
/patches/
